for bs, v in sz["edge_80pct_at_buy_share"].items(): w(f"| {pc(float(bs), 0)} | {n(v['buys'])} | {pc(v['sd_march'], 2)} | {pc(v['sd_march_x1_5'], 2)} |")
e = sz["events_for_80pct_at_0_5pct_edge_half_bought"]
w(f"\nTo show a 0.5% edge 80% of the time when half the events are bought: about {n(e['sd_march'])} held-back events at the March sd, {n(e['sd_march_x1_5'])} at 1.5 times. Events cluster in the same weeks and share the market's move, so the real sample behaves as fewer independent bets and these edges are a lower bound.\n")
w("The design sample is smaller. With " + n(ds["events"]) + " events, a set-up's edge has a standard error several times larger than on the held-back half, so the choice among six set-ups is mostly noise unless their true edges differ by about 1% or more. That is a weak choice, not a biased one: the held-back half is read by one set-up only.\n")
w("## Extraction check (MEASURED by `extract_check_11.py`; reading notes in `EXTRACTION_CHECK_11.md`)\n")
t = X["totals"]
w(f"- {t['texts']} texts, a seeded pick across the three lists, against each filing's raw exhibit. Number tokens kept: {pc(t['mean_numbers_kept_share'], 1)} on average, lowest {pc(t['lowest_numbers_kept_share'], 1)}. Texts with images (charts, logos), which a reader never sees: {t['with_images']}. Cut by the cap: {t['cut']}. Guidance heading found: {t['guidance_heading_found']}, before the cap: {t['guidance_before_cap']}.")
w("- The first extractor split words wherever a style tag sat mid-word and broke table rows. It was replaced on 2026-11-25, before any reader saw a text (`fetch_11_edgar.py`, extractor version 2). The check then found footnote marks glued to numbers (\"$0.81\" with footnote 1 read \"$0.811\"). Versions 3 to 5, the same day and still before any reader saw a text, write such marks as \"[1]\". All texts were re-extracted from the saved raw exhibits. Images (charts, logos, letter-style panels) are still lost.\n")
w("## How this can fool us\n")
w("- The code takes the close as 16:00 New York time on every trading day and does not know the early closes. No early close falls inside the event window (2026-04-01 to 2026-11-20), so no event is mislabeled by this. A later test with other dates must fix it first.")
w("- The open already prices the release. Most of the reaction is between the filing and the open, which is not counted. The test is whether anything is left.")
w("- The release is not the whole story. Guidance is often given on the call, after the release, and the open already holds the call.")
w("- Remembered context: the models know each company up to their line, including old guidance and consensus. That is public and fair, but stale by the later quarters. A model that remembers a 2026 event from before its line could get the direction from memory. The line was measured on crypto prices only.")
w("- Readers with tools (above). A reader that looked up the move would look skilled. The checker cannot see it.")
w("- Clustering: hundreds of events in the same few weeks. The random-pick yardstick draws from the same events, which helps, but the real sample is smaller than its count.")
w("- The cap loses the end of most texts (tables, definitions, the safe-harbor notice, and in a few texts part of the guidance), and the extraction loses anything shown only as an image. Footnote marks are kept as \"[1]\", which a reader must not take as a number.")
w("- Filing time is not release time. Many companies put the release on a newswire minutes before the 8-K is accepted. When the newswire is before 09:30 and the 8-K after it, entry is a day later than it could have been. This is counted by session and reported.")
w("- Costs for the 400 and the 600 are assumptions, not measured.")
w("- Choosing on the design sample is noisy (above). The 12 design trials are counted in the ledger all the same.")
w("- In `cascade`, the strong reader sees mixed folders on the design sample but only disagreements and the audit on the held-back half. The context differs, which can change its scores.\n")
